# Environment bindings and argument expansion

Assignments evaluate left to right; values keep whitespace and equal signs.

    :b printf (available)

    :envs FIRST=one SECOND=${FIRST}-two WORDS='two words' EQUAL=a=b EMPTY=
    :e COMMAND=printf
    :$ $COMMAND '<%s>\n' $FIRST ${SECOND} $WORDS $EQUAL $EMPTY $MISSING (exit: 0)

```stdout
<one>
<one-two>
<two words>
<a=b>
<>
<>
```

    :$ sh -c 'printf "<%s>\n" "$$SECOND" "$$WORDS"' (exit: 0)

```stdout
<one-two>
<two words>
```

Escaped dollars belong to the child shell, including positional parameters.
Expansion is single-pass, without command substitution or word splitting.

    :envs LITERAL='$$FIRST' CODE='$(echo not-executed)'
    :$ printf '<%s>\n' $LITERAL $CODE $$FIRST end$ (exit: 0)

```stdout
<$FIRST>
<$(echo not-executed)>
<$FIRST>
<end$>
```

    :$ sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "$$1"' sh 'positional value' (exit: 0)

```stdout
positional value
```

Sandbox defaults are visible without inheriting arbitrary host environment.
Overriding HOME changes the child environment, not the sandbox working directory.

    :$ sh -c 'test "$HOME" = "$$HOME" && test "$PATH" = "$$PATH" && test "$ENV" = "$$ENV" && test "$SHELL" = "$$SHELL" && test "$LC_CTYPE" = "$$LC_CTYPE" && test "$TMUX" = "$$TMUX" && test "$TMUX_PANE" = "$$TMUX_PANE" && echo defaults-match' (exit: 0)

```stdout
defaults-match
```

    :envs HOME=custom TMUX=custom-socket TMUX_PANE=custom-pane
    :$ sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "$$HOME" "$$TMUX" "$$TMUX_PANE"' (exit: 0)

```stdout
custom
custom-socket
custom-pane
```

    :$ printf '%s\n' $HOME $TMUX $TMUX_PANE (exit: 0)

```stdout
custom
custom-socket
custom-pane
```

    :e -u HOME TMUX TMUX_PANE SECOND
    :$ sh -c 'printf "<%s>\n" "$${HOME-unset}" "$${TMUX-unset}" "$${TMUX_PANE-unset}" "$${SECOND-unset}"' (exit: 0)

```stdout
<unset>
<unset>
<unset>
<unset>
```

A failed pane selection preserves an override. A successful selection replaces
it, or restores an unset value. Other removed defaults stay absent, and ordinary
tmux commands do not restore the pane binding.

    :e TMUX_PANE=override
    :p no-such-pane

> [!WARNING]
> No such pane.

    :$ printf '%s\n' $TMUX_PANE (exit: 0)

```stdout
override
```

    :t split-window -d (success)
    :p 0.1
    :$ sh -c 'test "$TMUX_PANE" = "$$TMUX_PANE" && printf "%s\n" "$$TMUX_PANE" "$${TMUX-unset}" "$${HOME-unset}"' (exit: 0)

```stdout
%1
unset
unset
```

    :e -u TMUX_PANE
    :t display-message -p constant (success)

```
constant
```

    :$ sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "$${TMUX_PANE-unset}"' (exit: 0)

```stdout
unset
```

    :p 0.0
    :$ sh -c 'test "$TMUX_PANE" = "$$TMUX_PANE" && printf "%s\n" "$$TMUX_PANE"' (exit: 0)

```stdout
%0
```

Exports affect subsequent host commands, not the running tmux server.

    :envs ONLY_CHILD=present
    :t show-environment -g ONLY_CHILD (failure)

```
unknown variable: ONLY_CHILD
```

    :$ sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "$$ONLY_CHILD"' (exit: 0)

```stdout
present
```

Malformed assignments warn independently; valid later assignments still apply.

    :envs INVALID =missing GOOD=kept BROKEN='${OPEN' AFTER=$GOOD

> [!WARNING]
> invalid assignment 'INVALID': expected NAME=VALUE

> [!WARNING]
> invalid assignment '=missing': invalid variable name ''

> [!WARNING]
> invalid assignment 'BROKEN=${OPEN': unterminated variable reference

    :$ printf '%s\n' $AFTER (exit: 0)

```stdout
kept
```

    :envs

> [!WARNING]
> Parser error: error: the following required arguments were not provided:
>   <ARGS>...
>
> Usage: :envs <ARGS>...
>
> For more information, try '--help'.
>

    :$ printf '%s\n' '${OPEN' (failed)

> [!WARNING]
> unterminated variable reference

